    "media:imagetext": "and Woods as passengers, from Hankor on Wednesday the 18th\nwith orders to proceed to Canton though we rather hoped\nthat he would go to Hongkong.\non approaching Canton our\nagent there redioed the plane that conditions at Canton\nwere bad and advised Havelick to continue on to Hongkong.\nAfter the arrival at Hongkong we hoped the pilot would report\nengine or other trouble but hearing none we ordered him baok\nto Hankow over the Company radio while sending him an urgent\ncode message to remein until receipt of instratione in a\nprivate code which I have with besener.\nManager of\nKai Tak Airport, wired to all statione north\nincladine\nShanghai of course that Havellok would be held until receipt\nof contidential instructione from me. You can imagine how\npleasant it was around the office atter that! Well, anyway\nup to the time of leeving shanghai we had managed to hold the\nehip at Hong Kong and I have spent many hours trying to think\nup some scheme to get that D0-2 over to manila without aotu-\nally committing an act of horee stealing shich I assume might\nlead to complications not only at Nanking but possibly at\nWashington and certainly at Hong Kong where we would have\nto clear tor'a certain destination.\nI thought of clearing\ntor Hankow and getting lost but the direotion and terrein\nare both quite different.\nI received your gable #110 today\non ship board and noted your suggestion that I consult a\nLawyer in Hong Kong as to ways and means of holding the ship.\nI have already done so at hanghai and the lawyer could\noffer no suggestion except that there wee no legal way to\naccomplish what I wanted to do.\nPoseibly the letter wiich I wrote to the Managing D1-\nreotor on Ang, 28rd will turninh the basis for a little\nstrategy which I had in mind at the time of making the pro-\nposal and which I have dieonased with no one except two of\nthe pilots who are with me on this boat.\nstricteet contidence, and for obvious ressons, o11 the\nOur new Managing Director has a bad reputation and I\ndoubt 1i his own people will belleve him in preference to\nme as with all proper modesty I believe my reputation for\nintegrity, up to the preeent time at least, la fairly good.\nI propose to write the Menaging Director a letter confirming\nhis acceptence of our offer of aug. 23rd to oredit UNAC\nwith $60,000.00 U.S. for the D0-2.\nThis contirming letter,\nwith a copy of my letter of ang. 28rd, I will exhibit to\nMose as evidence of our ownership.\nThe only man who can\ndeny the deal is the Managing Director and no one will be-\nlieve him.\nIf Moss will olear the DO-Z on my say so then\nwe are in the poeition where possession is nine tenthe of\nthe law.\nNaturally you are to know nothing of this deal\no1tielally.\nI belleve we are juetified in taking the 10-2\nbecause ONA oves us more than $60,000,00 U.S. and won't\nIn making\nthis statement I am assuming\nof course that\nONAO takes the two Dolphins.\nup to the time I left Shang-\nhai those shipe were still eafe and eo well camollaged that\neven the Jap air photos do not reveal their whereaboute.\nI hope ONA takes the volphine beause we couldn't fly them\nto Hong Kong even 11 pilote were willing to try. Hangohow\nBey is full of Japsnese boats and planes.\nA Jap airplane\ncarrier is at rooshow and Jap deetroyers at Anoy and Swatow.\nIf we tried to fly them to Bankow, and the Chinese would\nlet them get away, which de doubtful, we would have to make\na landing between\nHenkow and Hong Kong and there is no fuel\nor proper place to land.\none of our good chinese friende\nin the Operations Department has promieed me faithfully to\npaint out the U,S. Dept, 02 Commerce zambers.",
